  }
});
```

Once you switch to the promise-based API and load the image after the style is ready, the icons will appear again. 🎉

### TL;DR for the two things you asked about  

| Mapbox GL JS v2 code | What you must change for MapLibre GL JS (v6-latest) |
|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| `new mapboxgl.Map({ preserveDrawingBuffer: true, antialias: true, … })` | Move those flags into the **`canvasContextAttributes`** object. |
| `map.on('load', a).on('click', b)` | `map.on` now returns a **subscription object**, so the chain breaks – call `on` separately for each event (or keep the returned subscription if you need to later `remove`). |

Below is a minimal, working example that shows the new pattern.

---

## 1️⃣ Canvas-context options (`preserveDrawingBuffer`, `antialias`)

In MapLibre GL JS v5+ the top-level map options were split; any WebGL context flags must be supplied under `canvasContextAttributes`.

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12,

  // ← NEW place for WebGL context flags
  canvasContextAttributes: {
    preserveDrawingBuffer: true, // keep the canvas after rendering (e.g. for screenshots)
    antialias: true               // smoother lines / polygons
  },

  // other options you may already have (e.g. maxZoom, minZoom, etc.)
});
```

> **Why?**  
> Starting with MapLibre GL JS v5 the map constructor no longer reads `preserveDrawingBuffer` or `antialias` from the root options object. If you keep them where they were, they are silently ignored and you’ll see no difference (or you may get a warning in the console).

---

## 2️⃣ Event-listener chaining

```js
// ❌ This no longer works – `on` returns a subscription, not the map
// map.on('load', a).on('click', b);

// ✅ Call `on` separately (or keep the subscription if you need to remove it later)
map.on('load', a);
map.on('click', b);

// If you *do* need the subscription (e.g. to later call .remove()), keep it:
const loadSub = map.on('load', a);
// later: loadSub.remove();
```

> **What changed?**  
> In MapLibre GL JS v5 the `on` method was altered to return a **`MapLibreEventSubscription`** object so you can later call `.remove()`. Because the return value is no longer the map instance, the classic “fluent” chaining pattern breaks.

---

## 3️⃣ Quick checklist – other things you’ll probably need to touch in a v2 → MapLibre migration

| Step | What to do | Why it matters |
|------|------------|----------------|
| **Package & import** | `npm uninstall mapbox-gl && npm install maplibre-gl`  <br> `import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` | Swaps the library and the namespace (`mapboxgl → maplibregl`). |
| **Access token** | Delete any `mapboxgl.accessToken = '…'` line. | MapLibre does **not** use Mapbox tokens. |
| **Style URL** | Replace `style: 'mapbox://styles/…'` with a non-Mapbox style (e.g. OpenFreeMap, your own TileJSON, or a style you exported from Mapbox Studio and rewired). | `mapbox://` URLs only work with Mapbox services. |
| **CSS class names** | Change any `mapboxgl-…` class you reference in your own CSS to `maplibregl-…`. | The generated control markup now uses the `maplibregl-` prefix. |
| **Callback → Promise** | Methods like `map.loadImage`, `source.getClusterChildren`, etc., now return a **Promise** instead of accepting a callback. | If you still pass a callback you’ll get a runtime error. |
| **v2-only APIs** | `map.setFog` → `map.setSky` (or drop it). <br> Free-camera methods → `map.calculateCameraOptionsFromCameraLngLatAltRotation(... )` + `map.jumpTo/easeTo/flyTo`. | Those methods never existed in MapLibre; they were added only in Mapbox v2. |
| **Plugins** | Verify each plugin you use: <br> • `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw` works after a small CSS rename (or use `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-draw`). <br> • `mapbox-gl-compare` → `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-compare`. <br> • Any plugin that calls Mapbox REST APIs (geocoding, directions, etc.) must be swapped for an open-source alternative. | Plugins that depend on Mapbox services will still try to hit `api.mapbox.com` and return 401. |

---

## 4️⃣ Full minimal example (combining everything)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre migration demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12,
    canvasContextAttributes: {
      preserveDrawingBuffer: true,
      antialias: true
    }
  });

  // Separate listeners (no chaining)
  map.on('load', () => console.log('map loaded'));
  map.on('click', e => console.log('clicked at', e.lngLat));
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Run that in a modern browser (MapLibre v6 requires WebGL 2). If the map appears, you’ve successfully handled the two issues you asked about. From there you can continue the rest of the migration checklist.

---

### Got more questions?

* **“My style still shows a blank map”** – check for any lingering `mapbox://` URLs (style, sprite, glyphs, source URLs).  
* **“I’m using a Mapbox plugin that crashes”** – tell me the exact plugin name and I’ll point you to the MapLibre-compatible fork or alternative.  
* **“I need to keep `preserveDrawingBuffer` for screenshots, but it hurts performance”** – you can toggle it at runtime by recreating the map with a different `canvasContextAttributes` object, or use `map.getCanvas().toDataURL()` after the map has rendered (still requires the flag).  

Feel free to paste any error messages or snippets that still don’t work, and I’ll help you troubleshoot the next step. Happy mapping!

Below is a quick-reference guide that covers **all practical ways to add a search / geocoder box** when you are already using **Mapbox GL JS v2**.  
It is split into two sections:

| ✅ If you stay on Mapbox GL JS v2 (you have a Mapbox access token) | ✅ If you are planning to move to MapLibre GL JS (or you want a fully open-source stack) |
|---|---|
| Use the official Mapbox Geocoder plugin (the simplest, but it ties you to Mapbox services). | Use the MapLibre-compatible fork of the same plugin, or roll your own UI that talks to an open geocoder (Nominatim, Photon, Pelias, MapTiler, etc.). |
| You can also embed a third-party UI (Algolia Places, GeoSearch, etc.) that talks to any HTTP geocoder you like. | Same UI options work; just point them at a different endpoint. |

---

## 1️⃣ Staying on Mapbox GL JS v2 – the “official” way

### 1.1 Install the official plugin

```bash
npm i @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder
# or, if you use a CDN:
# <script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.min.js"></script>
# <link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css" rel="stylesheet" />
```

> **Version note** – The latest `v4.x` release works with Mapbox GL JS v2.  
> If you are on an older v2.x (e.g. 2.0-2.6) you can still use `v4.4.0` – it was the last version that supported the older `mapboxgl.accessToken`-based workflow.

### 1.2 Basic usage

```javascript
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';
import MapboxGeocoder from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder';
import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css';

mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN';

const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12', // any Mapbox style
  center: [-122.44, 37.76],
  zoom: 12
});

const geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({
  accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken,   // <-- required
  mapboxgl: mapboxgl,                  // <-- makes the control aware of the map instance
  placeholder: 'Search places …',
  // optional: limit results to a country, bias to a viewport, etc.
  //   countries: 'us,ca',
  //   bbox: [-123, 36, -121, 38],
});

map.addControl(geocoder, 'top-left');   // or 'top-right', 'bottom-left', …
```

**What you get out of the box**

| Feature | How it works |
|---|---|
| **Autocomplete** | Calls Mapbox Geocoding API (`https://api.mapbox.com/geocoding/v5/mapbox.places/...`) and shows suggestions as the user types. |
| **Result handling** | The control automatically flies the map to the selected result (`map.flyTo({center: result.center, zoom: 14})`). You can listen to `result` events if you need custom logic: `geocoder.on('result', e => console.log(e.result));` |
| **Reverse geocoding** | Not built-in, but you can call the same API with `reverseGeocode: true` if you need it. |
| **Country / proximity bias** | Pass `countries`, `proximity`, `bbox`, `types`, `autocomplete: true/false`, etc. in the options object. |
| **Custom UI** | You can embed the geocoder in any DOM element: `new MapboxGeocoder({container: '#my-search'})`. |
| **Styling** | The CSS file ships with a `.mapboxgl-ctrl-geocoder` class; you can override it in your own stylesheet. |

### 1.3 Usage policies & limits

| Item | Details |
|---|---|
| **Attribution** | Must display “© Mapbox, © OpenStreetMap contributors”. The plugin automatically adds the required attribution to the map’s attribution control. |
| **Rate limits** | Free tier: 100 000 requests/month, 600 req/min. Paid plans raise the limits. |
| **Terms of service** | The Geocoding API is a **Mapbox-only** service; you cannot proxy it through a third-party server without explicit permission. |
| **Token security** | The token is sent client-side, so keep it **public-read-only** (e.g., a token with only the `geocoding:search` scope). Do **not** embed a secret token. |

---

## 2️⃣ Moving to MapLibre GL JS (or you want an open-source geocoder)

If you ever plan to replace Mapbox GL JS with MapLibre, the **official Mapbox plugin will not work** because it hard-codes the `mapbox://` endpoint and expects a Mapbox token. The good news is that the MapLibre community has created a **drop-in fork** plus a set of open geocoder services you can use.

### 2.1 The MapLibre-compatible fork

| Package | Repo | Install |
|---|---|---|
| `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` | <https://github.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder> | `npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` |

> **Why a fork?** The original plugin imports `mapboxgl` directly and builds the request URL with `https://api.mapbox.com/...`. The fork replaces those imports with a generic `maplibregl` reference and lets you pass any **Geocoding endpoint** you like.

### 2.2 Example with **Nominatim** (the free OSM geocoder)

```bash
npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder
npm i maplibre-gl   # if you haven’t already
```

```javascript
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css';

// 1️⃣  Choose a style that does NOT use mapbox:// URLs
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any public style
  center: [-122.44, 37.76],
  zoom: 12
});

// 2️⃣  Create the geocoder – point it at a Nominatim endpoint
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // No access token needed for Nominatim
  placeholder: 'Search OSM places …',
  // The endpoint can be the public demo server (low-volume only) or your own host
  //   (recommended for production)
  //   e.g. 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=geojson&addressdetails=1'
  //   or a self-hosted instance at https://my-nominatim.example.com/search
  //   The plugin expects the endpoint to return GeoJSON FeatureCollection.
  //   If you use the public demo, add a `User-Agent` header via fetch-intercept
  //   (or use a proxy) because Nominatim blocks generic browsers.
  //   The plugin ships a tiny wrapper that adds `&format=geojson` automatically.
  //   You can also set `params: {countrycodes: 'us,ca'}` to limit results.
  //   See the plugin README for all options.
  //   If you have a MapTiler Geocoding key, you can use:
  //   endpoint: 'https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/{query}.json?key=YOUR_KEY'
  endpoint: 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search',
  // Optional: bias results to the current map viewport
  //   (the plugin will add `viewbox` and `bounded=1` automatically)
  //   If you want a static bias, set `proximity: [lon, lat]`.
  //   If you want to limit to a country, set `params: {countrycodes: 'us'}`
  params: {
    addressdetails: 1,
    limit: 5,
    // countrycodes: 'us,ca'   // uncomment to restrict
  },
  // Optional: custom result rendering (HTML)
  // render: (feature) => `<strong>${feature.properties.display_name}</strong>`
});

map.addControl(geocoder, 'top-left');
```

#### What the fork does differently

| Mapbox plugin | MapLibre fork |
|---|---|
| `accessToken` (required) | **No token needed** – you supply an `endpoint` URL. |
| Hard-coded request URL (`https://api.mapbox.com/geocoding/v5/...`) | Uses the `endpoint` you give, appends the query string, and expects **GeoJSON**. |
| CSS class prefix `mapboxgl-` | Uses the same CSS (the class name stays `mapboxgl-ctrl-geocoder` for compatibility). You can still override it. |
| Only works with Mapbox GL JS | Works with any `maplibregl`-compatible map (MapLibre, MapLibre-GL-JS v6+, or even the older `maplibre-gl@1` compatibility branch). |

### 2.3 Other open geocoder services you can plug into the same component

| Service | Endpoint pattern (replace `{query}`) | Rate-limit / policy notes |
|---|---|---|
| **MapTiler Geocoding** | `https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/{query}.json?key=YOUR_KEY` | Free tier 100 k req/mo, commercial plans available. |
| **Pelias** (hosted by many providers) | `https://search.geocode.earth/v1/autocomplete?text={query}&api_key=YOUR_KEY` | Free tier 2 k req/mo on geocode.earth; self-hostable. |
| **Photon** (open-source, hosted by Komoot) | `https://photon.komoot.io/api/?q={query}` | No hard limits, but **rate-limit politely** (≤ 1 req/s). |
| **OpenCage** | `https://api.opencagedata.com/geocode/v1/json?q={query}&key=YOUR
